--csharp
    static void Main() {
        var rt = new Rt();
        rt.boxes[0] = 9007199254740993L; rt.boxes[1] = 9007199254740992L;
        var o = new StringBuilder();
        long[] vs = { Fix(3), Fix(-7), Big(0), Dbl(3), 0L };
        foreach (long v in vs) {
            o.Append(System.BitConverter.DoubleToInt64Bits(NumberF64(rt, v)).ToString("X16")).Append(' ');
        }
        o.Append('|');
        long[][] ps = { new long[]{Fix(1), Fix(2)}, new long[]{Fix(2), Fix(1)},
                        new long[]{Fix(2), Fix(2)}, new long[]{Big(0), Big(1)},
                        new long[]{Big(1), Big(0)}, new long[]{Fix(1), Dbl(3)},
                        new long[]{0L, Fix(1)}, new long[]{Fix(1), 0L} };
        foreach (long[] p in ps) o.Append(' ').Append(NumberCmp(rt, p[0], p[1]));
        System.Console.WriteLine(o.ToString());
    }
--expect
4008000000000000 C01C000000000000 4340000000000000 3FF8000000000000 7FF8000000000000 | -1 1 0 1 -1 -1 0 0
--expect-why
DERIVED from IEEE 754 and the fixture's tagging, not recorded from a run.
The top nibble is the tier: 1 fixnum, 2 double, 3 bigint, anything else not a
number. A double holds twice its value in the low byte, so `dbl(3)` is 1.5.

FIVE BIT PATTERNS, printed as bits because the last one cannot be printed any
other way: NaN is not equal to itself, so a probe that compared doubles would
report agreement no matter which NaN each runtime produced.

  fix(3)   -> 3.0                sign 0, exp 1024, mantissa .5    4008000000000000
  fix(-7)  -> -7.0               sign 1, exp 1025, mantissa .75   C01C000000000000
  big(0)   -> 9007199254740993   rounds to 2^53 as a double       4340000000000000
  dbl(3)   -> 1.5                sign 0, exp 1023, mantissa .5    3FF8000000000000
  Value(0) -> not a number       CANONICAL_NAN                    7FF8000000000000

THE FIFTH IS THE WHOLE FILE. Each runtime used to answer this with its own
host constant, and `double.NaN` on .NET is FFF8000000000000 -- the negative
quiet NaN -- where `f64::NAN` and `Double.NaN` are both 7FF8000000000000.
Measured on all three hosts rather than argued from the specs. Arithmetic NaN
is NOT the divergence: `0.0/0.0`, `sqrt(-1)` and `inf-inf` give
7FF8000000000000 everywhere, so a probe built from computed NaNs would have
passed while the named constant stayed wrong.

The third row is the fifth's quieter companion. `9007199254740993` is not
representable, so the conversion rounds, and the row pins WHICH WAY: to even,
giving 2^53 rather than 2^53 + 2. A runtime that truncated would print
433FFFFFFFFFFFFF and no equality test would notice, because the value is only
ever compared against other doubles that rounded the same way.

EIGHT COMPARISONS. The first three are the ordinary integer ordering, less,
greater and equal, and they would pass on either path.

  (1, 2)                -1      (2, 1)                 1      (2, 2)      0

The FOURTH AND FIFTH are the pair that require the integer path to exist:
9007199254740993 against 9007199254740992 is 1, and the reverse is -1. Both
convert to the SAME double, 9007199254740992.0, so a `number-cmp` that
promoted first would answer 0 to both and be wrong about two integers that
differ by one. This is the case the `Option<i64>` removal could have broken
and did not -- `is-int` and `i64-of` together do what the nullable did.

  (2^53+1, 2^53)         1      (2^53, 2^53+1)        -1

The SIXTH is the mixed pair, one integer and one double, which must NOT take
the integer path: 1.0 against 1.5 is -1. Without it a source could answer
every row above by comparing integers alone and never promote anything.

  (1, 1.5)              -1

The LAST TWO are NaN's ordering, in both argument positions. NaN is neither
less than nor greater than 1.0, so both comparisons fall through to 0 -- NaN
sorts as EQUAL to everything, which is what `Double.compare` does inside
Clojure's `compare` and what native already did. Both positions are present
because a source that tested only `p < q` and only in one order would get one
of them right by accident.

  (NaN, 1)               0      (1, NaN)               0
